19:T77f,## The fat-interface smell

The **I** in SOLID: *no client should be forced to depend on methods it doesn’t use.*

When one interface tries to describe **everything** a participant might do, it becomes a **fat interface**. A class that only needs a slice of it is still handed the whole thing, and the compiler insists it provide a body for every method, even the ones that make no sense for it.

```
interface Worker {
    void work();
    void eat();
    void sleep();
}
```

## Empty / throwing methods

A `RobotWorker` can `work()`, but it doesn’t `eat()` or `sleep()`. Forced to implement them anyway, you end up writing **stubs that lie**:

```
class RobotWorker implements Worker {
    public void work()  { /* real */ }
    public void eat()   { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }
    public void sleep() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }
}
```

Those empty/throwing bodies are the smell. Callers can’t trust the interface, `worker.eat()` might blow up at runtime.

## One change ripples to unrelated clients

Worse, the interface now **couples** every implementer. Change `eat()`’s signature for the humans and **RobotWorker stops compiling** too, a class that never cared about eating is dragged into the recompile. A trivial edit breaks unrelated code.

## Split by ROLE

The fix is to **segregate** the fat interface into small, role-specific ones, each describing a single capability:

- `Workable` → `work()`
- `Eatable` → `eat()`
- `Sleepable` → `sleep()`

A class then picks **à la carte**: it implements only the roles it actually fulfils. `HumanWorker` takes all three; `RobotWorker` takes just `Workable`. Each class depends on nothing it doesn’t use, and a change to one role can’t reach a class that never implemented it.

> A class **may implement several** small interfaces, segregation costs nothing here, since one type can wear many role hats at once.c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-solid","activeLesson":"interface-segregation"}],["$","$L18","interface-segregation",{"lesson":{"id":"interface-segregation","patternId":"lld-solid","title":"Interface Segregation (I)","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Core","statement":"No client should be forced to depend on methods it doesn’t use. Prefer many small, role-specific interfaces over one fat one.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Microsoft"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"The fat interface","code":["interface Worker {","    void work();","    void eat();","    void sleep();","}","class HumanWorker implements Worker { ... }","class RobotWorker implements Worker {","    public void work()  { /* real */ }","    public void eat()   { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }","    public void sleep() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException(); }","}"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Start with ONE big Worker interface bundling three capabilities together: work() + eat() + sleep(). Every implementer must supply all three.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"worker","name":"Worker","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ work()","+ eat()","+ sleep()"],"x":1,"y":0.2,"tint":"red"},{"id":"human","name":"HumanWorker","methods":["+ work()","+ eat()","+ sleep()"],"x":0,"y":1.6},{"id":"robot","name":"RobotWorker","methods":["+ work()","+ eat() ✗","+ sleep() ✗"],"x":2,"y":1.6,"tint":"red"}],"relations":[{"id":"h","from":"human","to":"worker","kind":"implement"},{"id":"r","from":"robot","to":"worker","kind":"implement"}],"shownClassIds":["worker"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["worker"]},"vars":[["Worker","work + eat + sleep"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":6,"narration":"HumanWorker implements Worker. A human genuinely works, eats and sleeps, so filling in all three methods is fine, no friction here.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["worker","human"],"shownRelIds":["h"],"activeIds":["human"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":7,"narration":"But RobotWorker only works, it has no use for eat()/sleep(). Forced to implement them anyway, it throws UnsupportedOperation. Those lying stubs are the violation, and changing eat() now recompiles the robot too.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["worker","human","robot"],"shownRelIds":["h","r"],"activeIds":["robot"]},"vars":[["Robot","forced to fake eat/sleep 💥"]]}]},{"label":"Segregate by role","code":["interface Workable  { void work();  }","interface Eatable   { void eat();   }","interface Sleepable { void sleep(); }","","class HumanWorker implements Workable, Eatable, Sleepable { ... }","class RobotWorker implements Workable { ... }"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Split the fat Worker into three role interfaces: Workable, Eatable, Sleepable, each a single focused capability. A class now picks only the roles it actually fulfils.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"workable","name":"Workable","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ work()"],"x":0.3,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"eatable","name":"Eatable","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ eat()"],"x":1.2,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"sleepable","name":"Sleepable","stereotype":"interface","methods":["+ sleep()"],"x":2.1,"y":0.2,"tint":"accent"},{"id":"human","name":"HumanWorker","x":0.5,"y":1.7},{"id":"robot","name":"RobotWorker","x":1.9,"y":1.7,"tint":"green"}],"relations":[{"id":"hw","from":"human","to":"workable","kind":"implement"},{"id":"he","from":"human","to":"eatable","kind":"implement"},{"id":"hs","from":"human","to":"sleepable","kind":"implement"},{"id":"rw","from":"robot","to":"workable","kind":"implement"}],"shownClassIds":["workable","eatable","sleepable"],"shownRelIds":[],"activeIds":["workable","eatable","sleepable"]},"vars":[["split into","3 role interfaces"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":5,"narration":"HumanWorker implements all three, it really does work, eat and sleep. A single class can implement MANY small interfaces, so segregation costs it nothing.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["workable","eatable","sleepable","human"],"shownRelIds":["hw","he","hs"],"activeIds":["human"]}},{"view":"class","codeLine":6,"narration":"RobotWorker implements ONLY Workable. No empty methods, no throwing stubs, it depends on nothing it doesn’t use, and a change to Eatable can never reach it.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["workable","eatable","sleepable","human","robot"],"shownRelIds":["hw","he","hs","rw"],"activeIds":["robot"]},"vars":[["Robot","implements Workable only ✓"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"The rule: prefer small, focused interfaces, implemented à la carte. Each client depends only on the methods it uses, and unrelated changes can’t break it.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["workable","eatable","sleepable","human","robot"],"shownRelIds":["hw","he","hs","rw"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["small interfaces","à la carte ✓"]]}]}]}}]]}]
